Dear ACs and reviewers,

We appreciate the time spent on preparing detailed reviews. We have provided responses to all reviewers, clarified details of the method, and added novel experimental results that support our contributions. The important aspect of the ICLR reviewing process is collaborative discussion and improvement of the paper. We would like to ask reviewers to provide feedback on the rebuttal and revise scores accordingly.

Additionally we would like to emphasize technical and experimental novelties of this paper:

1. We provide an effective method to prune ViT model, packing the information learned by the full model into a small, highly efficient pruned model. The pruned model achieves both better efficiency and better transferability to downstream tasks compared to other SOTA methods and DEIT counterparts;

2. We show the techniques like explicit head alignment, Taylor-based global pruning, latency regularization and full model distillation works well together leading to the high efficiency of our pruned model;

3. We show the potential of finding novel scalable parameter redistribution rules via the global pruning of the ViT model, and provide a set of simple design rules leading to the NViT family, which always achieve higher accuracy with a lower latency compared to DEIT models.

In the responses, we provide following additional results to further support our contributions:

1. Ablation study results on why magnitude-based pruning is not preferable; 

2. Ablation study results on the effect of latency regularization on achieving higher accuracy and finding more efficient parameter redistribution rule

3. Pruned model performance and architecture of applying our pruning method on the stage 2 of the SWIN-B model, with analysis on the potential inspiration on the parameter redistribution rule.
